# <project-name> — web-pwa

<!-- Audit: B.9.2 (b9-2-web-pwa) -->

The installable **Qwik City PWA** surface of `<project-name>`, the default channel
for Web, Android and desktop. On iOS, when push delivery is critical, the native
Flutter `app` surface at the repository root is the prescribed fallback
(`docs/ARCHITECTURE-TARGET.md` §6.3).

Governed by `.forge/standards/web-frontend.yaml` (framework choice) and
`.forge/standards/pwa.yaml` (installability, offline behaviour, push delivery).

## Commands

```bash
npm install
npm run dev        # vite --mode ssr
npm run build      # qwik build
npm run preview    # production preview (required to exercise the Service Worker)
```

## ⚠️ PITFALL — do not upgrade Vite past 7.x

`vite` is pinned **exactly**. Vite 8.x is the npm `latest` (8.1.5 at 2026-07-28) but
is **excluded** by `@builder.io/qwik` `peerDependencies` `">=5 <8"`, re-confirmed
live on 2026-07-28. Installing the default `vite` yields an incompatible peer and a
broken build. Re-check the peer range before changing this pin.

## ⚠️ Push notifications do not work out of the box — this is by design

This archetype is `layer_profile: client-only`: it has **no backend layer**. The
scaffold therefore ships only the **client half** of Web Push — subscription and
notification display. It contains no push server, no VAPID key generation and no
key storage, because all three are backend concerns (ADR-B9-2-005).

To make push actually deliver you must:

1. Generate a VAPID key pair yourself, and keep the private key **out of this repo**.
2. Expose the public key to the build as `PUBLIC_VAPID_KEY`.
3. Run your own sender that receives the `PushSubscription` from
   `src/lib/push.ts` and pushes to it.

Until you do, `subscribeToPush()` succeeds and no notification ever arrives.

## Offline behaviour

`src/routes/service-worker.ts` precaches `/offline/` on install and serves it for
any failed navigation, so the app never shows a browser network-error page. Keep
the offline route dependency-free: anything it imports must be precached too, or
the shell breaks exactly when it is needed.

## No backend client here

Unlike the flagship `frontend/web-public/` surface, this one ships **no**
`@connectrpc/*` dependency and no `src/lib/connect-client.ts`. There is nothing to
call. If a future change gives this archetype a backend, adding a Connect client is
purely additive.
